---
import QuestionLayout from '@layouts/QuestionLayout.astro';

const jsonLd = JSON.stringify({
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [{
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: 'Can I migrate existing business logic into Traverse?',
    acceptedAnswer: {
      '@type': 'Answer',
      text: "Yes, in the sense that you re-express the logic as a Traverse capability (WASM + contract) — Traverse doesn't ingest existing code from another language automatically. Usual authoring path: plain English via traverse-capability-author (under the hood still Rust→WASM). Manual authoring still uses Rust→WASM. Pick one duplicated or hard-to-audit rule, publish that capability, and point callers at it — not a whole-codebase migration.",
    },
  }],
});

const relatedLinks = [
  { href: '/blog/logic-duplication.html', label: 'Why I stopped writing the same business logic four times' },
  { href: '/questions/how-do-i-write-a-capability-contract.html', label: 'How do I write a capability contract?' },
  { href: '/questions/do-i-need-rust.html', label: 'Do I need to know Rust to use Traverse?' },
  { href: '/docs/quickstart.html', label: 'Quickstart' },
];
---
<QuestionLayout
  title="Can I migrate existing business logic into Traverse?"
  description="Yes — re-express one rule as a Traverse capability (skill-first plain English, or manual Rust→WASM), one rule at a time. No automatic language converter; no whole-codebase migration."
  canonical="https://traverse-framework.com/questions/can-i-migrate-existing-business-logic-to-traverse.html"
  category="Question"
  relatedLinks={relatedLinks}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <p>There's no automatic converter that takes your existing JavaScript, Python, or C# business rule and produces a Traverse capability. You re-express the rule as a governed capability: usual path is the Claude skill <code>traverse-capability-author</code> in <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/claude-skills" target="_blank" rel="noopener">claude-skills</a> (plain English interview → registry check → contract → WASM → human-reviewed PR). Manual authoring still uses Rust→WASM. Either way, under the hood stays Rust→WASM for determinism, sandboxing, and portability. That's deliberate work, not a checkbox migration.</p>

  <p>Where it pays off is when you're not migrating "the codebase" but one specific rule that's currently duplicated, drifting, or hard to audit — a pricing calculation reimplemented in three services, an eligibility check nobody's fully sure is consistent across the app and the backend. You rewrite that one rule as a governed capability, define its contract precisely (including the edge cases the old scattered copies probably handled inconsistently), and update your existing callers to invoke the capability instead of their own local implementation.</p>

  <h2>A reasonable order of operations</h2>
  <p>Start with the rule that's causing the most actual pain from drift, not the most complex one. Get the contract clear first — inputs, outputs, preconditions, postconditions — since that step alone often surfaces inconsistencies between the existing copies. Then publish once (skill or manual Rust→WASM) and swap one caller over at a time, verifying the trace before removing the old local copy.</p>

  <p>You do not need to write Rust for the usual authoring or consume paths — see <a href="/questions/do-i-need-rust.html">do I need to know Rust to use Traverse</a> and <a href="/questions/how-do-i-author-a-capability-in-plain-english.html">author in plain English</a>. Manual authoring in the repo still expects Rust.</p>
</QuestionLayout>
